  "theory": {
    "intro": "The **power rule for integration** is $\\int x^{n}\\,dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C$ for every real $n \\ne -1$. Add one to the index, then divide by the new index. It works for negative and fractional indices too.",
    "facts": [
      "Add one to the index and divide by the new index: $\\int x^{n}\\,dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C$.",
      "The rule fails only at $n = -1$, because dividing by $n + 1$ would mean dividing by zero.",
      "A constant factor stays where it is: $\\int kx^{n}\\,dx = \\dfrac{kx^{n+1}}{n+1} + C$.",
      "A constant integrates to $kx$, since $\\int k\\,dx = \\int kx^{0}\\,dx = kx + C$.",
      "Rewrite $\\dfrac{1}{x^{m}}$ as $x^{-m}$ and $\\sqrt[m]{x^{p}}$ as $x^{\\frac{p}{m}}$ before applying the rule.",
      "For a definite integral, write $\\left[F(x)\\right]_{a}^{b}$ and compute $F(b) - F(a)$ in that order.",
      "A definite integral needs no $+C$, because the constant cancels in the subtraction.",
      "The value is negative wherever the curve lies below the $x$-axis across the interval.",
      "There is no product or quotient rule for integration, so expand brackets and split fractions first."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Rewrite every term as a power of $x$, expanding brackets or splitting fractions first.",
      "Add one to each index and divide by the new index.",
      "For an indefinite integral add $+C$; for a definite integral write $\\left[F(x)\\right]_{a}^{b}$.",
      "Substitute the upper limit and subtract the value at the lower limit."
    ]
  },
